<div class="ltx_dates">2018</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Crop diseases pose significant threats to global food security, agricultural productivity, and sustainable farming practices, directly affecting farmers’ livelihoods and economic stability. To address the growing need for effective crop disease management, AI-based disease alerting systems have emerged as promising tools by providing early detection and actionable insights for timely intervention. However, existing systems often overlook critical aspects such as data privacy, market pricing power, and farmer-friendly usability, leaving farmers vulnerable to privacy breaches and economic exploitation. To bridge these gaps, we propose AgriSentinel, the first Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System. AgriSentinel incorporates a differential privacy mechanism to protect sensitive crop image data while maintaining classification accuracy. Its lightweight deep learning-based crop disease classification model is optimized for mobile devices, ensuring accessibility and usability for farmers. Additionally, the system includes a fine-tuned, on-device large language model (LLM) that leverages a curated knowledge pool to provide farmers with specific, actionable suggestions for managing crop diseases, going beyond simple alerting. Comprehensive experiments validate the effectiveness of AgriSentinel, demonstrating its ability to safeguard data privacy, maintain high classification performance, and deliver practical, actionable disease management strategies. AgriSentinel offers a robust, farmer-friendly solution for automating crop disease alerting and management, ultimately contributing to improved agricultural decision-making and enhanced crop productivity.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Large Language Model, Privacy and Security, Smart Agricultural, Mobile Computing
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Crop diseases are emerging as some of the greatest threats to global food security and agricultural productivity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ristaino et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, directly impacting farmers’ livelihoods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mehta et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, economic stability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and the broader goal of sustainable agriculture. To achieve crop disease alerting, there are increasing approaches to provide reliable tools to enhance farmers’ ability to manage crop health effectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">AI-based disease alerting systems offer a promising solution by providing early detection of crop diseases and actionable insights for timely intervention. By leveraging advanced AI technologies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nettleton et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tantalaki et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, such a system empowers farmers to make informed decisions that minimize crop losses, optimize yield, and reduce the reliance on excessive chemical inputs. This proactive approach not only improves productivity but also promotes sustainable agricultural practices by conserving resources, protecting the environment, and supporting long-term farming viability in the face of challenges such as pest outbreaks and changing environmental conditions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang and Shah, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mishra et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Moreover, with the emergence of large language models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Li, 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Li, 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Li, 2024c</a>)</cite>, these models excel at extracting structured information from unstructured data sources, including reports, news articles, and farmer records, providing actionable insights that enhance various agricultural tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Banerjee et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tzachor et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Furthermore, these models facilitate automated decision-making by integrating with other AI systems, streamlining the identification and management of crops and pests.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Unfortunately, these data-driven AI systems are vulnerable to malicious attacks, such as membership inference attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jalalzai et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, where attackers exploit the model to determine whether specific data points were used during training. In the context of agriculture, such attacks can expose sensitive crop data, including information about crop health, yield patterns, and regional farming practices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. This stolen data can then be used to predict crop production levels, allowing market players to manipulate pricing strategies to their advantage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sykuta, 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Javaid et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Such actions can disrupt fair market dynamics, leading to profit losses for farmers who are unable to compete effectively or secure equitable prices for their produce. Safeguarding crop data privacy is, therefore, essential to protect farmers’ economic interests and ensure a fair agricultural marketplace.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Although existing studies have explored AI-based crop disease alerting, they often lack a comprehensive focus on critical aspects such as privacy preservation to protect market pricing power and the development of farmer-friendly tools. This highlights the need for more holistic solutions that address both technical challenges and practical usability for farmers. To address this gap, we propose AgriSentinel, a Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System designed to achieve three key objectives: First, it incorporates a robust data privacy protection mechanism to safeguard sensitive crop data from potential misuse. Second, it features an on-device LLM-based model that not only provides farmers with specific strategic suggestions for managing crop diseases but also moves beyond simple alerting. Finally, its lightweight deep learning architecture is optimized for mobile devices, offering a more farmer-friendly and accessible solution to improve agricultural decision-making.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To sum up, this paper makes several significant contributions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to design a privacy-enhanced embedded-LLM crop disease alerting system.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose the AgriSentinel framework, which offers farmers a secure, user-friendly, LLM-based application for crop disease alerting and management.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A lightweight, differentially private deep learning-based crop disease classification model is developed within AgriSentinel, enabling accurate disease classification while safeguarding the privacy of crop image data.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In AgriSentinel, an on-device LLM-based model is fine-tuned to provide farmers with specific, actionable suggestions for managing crop diseases, utilizing a curated knowledge pool of crop disease information.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Comprehensive experiments are conducted to validate the effectiveness and practicality of the proposed AgriSentinel framework.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of this paper is organized as follows: Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Related Work ‣ AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> provides a brief overview of related work, while Section <a href="#S3" title="3. AgriSentinel Model ‣ AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents a detailed description of the proposed AgriSentinel framework. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Experiment ‣ AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we conduct real-world data experiments and analyze the results. Finally, the paper concludes with key insights in Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Conclusion ‣ AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section reviews the related works of crop disease alerting systems, agricultural large language models, and privacy-enhanced AI approaches.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Crop Disease Alerting Approaches</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Crop disease alerting approaches can be broadly categorized into context-aware models and data-driven models.
(1) Context-aware models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang and Shah, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mishra et al., 2021</a>)</cite> integrate broader contextual information, such as regional agricultural practices and environmental factors. For instance, real-time sensor inputs and historical weather data play a crucial role in improving the accuracy of disease forecasting. Additionally, social media data can provide insights into local agricultural sentiment, offering a holistic understanding of crop health dynamics.
(2) Data-driven models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nettleton et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tantalaki et al., 2019</a>)</cite> leverage diverse data sources, applying machine learning and deep learning techniques for disease prediction. CNNs have been widely used for classifying crop disease images <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chillakuru et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Techniques like feature-level fusion and ensemble learning enhance predictive accuracy by combining outputs from multiple data-driven models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jimenez et al., 2016</a>)</cite>. Furthermore, attention mechanisms improve the interpretability of extracted features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nong and Tarek, 2023</a>)</cite>, while representation learning aids in identifying critical patterns for precise crop disease prediction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Agricultural Large Language Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Modern LLMs like GPT and BERT have revolutionized data processing and generation with their exceptional understanding and adaptability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sapkota et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Recently, these models have begun transforming the agricultural sector by addressing complex challenges such as disease detection, crop monitoring, and strategic decision-making <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tzachor et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
In agriculture, LLMs excel at extracting structured information from unstructured data sources, including reports, news articles, and farmer records, providing actionable insights that enhance various agricultural tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Banerjee et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Furthermore, these models facilitate automated decision-making by integrating with other AI systems, streamlining the identification and management of crops and pests. Their few-shot learning capabilities are particularly advantageous in agriculture, where data collection is often time-intensive and costly.
However, implementing LLMs in agriculture poses significant challenges. These include the need for high-quality, diverse datasets and the computational resources required to train and deploy the models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Andreev et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Additionally, ethical concerns, such as data privacy and security, add further complexity to their practical adoption <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Silva et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Privacy-Enhanced AI Approaches</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Preserving data privacy is critical in the era of big data, enabling meaningful analysis and model training while safeguarding sensitive information. Four key methodologies are commonly employed for privacy protection in AI.
(1) Differential privacy, widely adopted in machine learning frameworks, introduces random noise to data or model outputs to prevent the identification of individual data points <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aggarwal et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
(2) Federated learning enhances privacy by training models across decentralized devices or servers holding local data, eliminating the need for data sharing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rahman et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
(3) Homomorphic encryption enables computations on encrypted data, ensuring sensitive information remains concealed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">El Mestari et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
(4) Secure multi-party computation (SMPC) facilitates privacy-preserving collaborative learning by allowing multiple parties to compute functions over private inputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gowda, 2024</a>)</cite>.
As the demand for privacy in data-driven applications grows, the integration of these techniques into AI pipelines is becoming increasingly vital to address risks associated with data breaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aouedi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The development of a privacy-enhanced LLM-based crop disease alerting system presents two significant technical challenges.
First, designing a lightweight and user-friendly system that allows farmers to easily adopt and leverage the transformative capabilities of large language models for effective crop disease management is inherently complex.
Second, ensuring privacy-enhanced training for agricultural LLMs is critical, as inadequately protected crop data could be exploited to infer production levels, potentially undermining farmers’ market pricing power and causing economic losses.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>AgriSentinel Model</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose an AgriSentinel framework, illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3. AgriSentinel Model ‣ AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, as a privacy-enhanced on-device system for comprehensive crop disease alerting that seamlessly integrates differential privacy, deep learning, and LLM.
The framework begins with a Gaussian noise-based differential privacy mechanism to safeguard sensitive crop data, introducing controlled noise to achieve a balance between privacy protection and the preservation of critical diagnostic features.
The obfuscated data is subsequently processed by a lightweight deep learning model designed for efficient and accurate disease classification, leveraging advanced architectural components such as convolutional layers and ReLU activation to optimize computational efficiency and predictive accuracy.
Building upon these predictive results, the framework incorporates a fine-tuned on-device LLM, enriched with a curated knowledge pool, to generate precise and actionable disease management strategies.
To sum up, AgriSentinel offers farmers a lightweight and user-friendly tool to enhance crop health management while addressing key challenges in usability and data protection.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09103v1/AgriSentinel.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/199;" width="476" height="199" alt="fig:AgriSentinel">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>AgriSentinel Framework<span id="acmlabel1" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">fig:AgriSentinel</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Differential Privacy Noise Injection Mechanism</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a differential privacy mechanism to protect crop data by employing Gaussian noise as an effective obfuscation technique. Gaussian noise introduces controlled random variations to the pixel values of an image, masking identifiable features while preserving the image’s utility for analysis. This approach strikes a balance between safeguarding data privacy and retaining the essential diagnostic information required for accurate disease pattern recognition. The process of applying Gaussian noise to an input image <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> can be mathematically expressed as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{obf}}=x+N(0,\sigma^{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>obf</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{obf}}=x+N(0,\sigma^{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{obf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>obf</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{obf}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the resulting obfuscated image and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Lap(0,\sigma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Lap(0,\sigma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> represents Gaussian noise with a mean of 0 and a tunable variance <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The mean value of 0 ensures that the noise is centered around the original pixel value, maintaining an unbiased perturbation, while the variance <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> dictates the level of noise introduced.
A higher variance results in more pronounced noise, providing stronger obfuscation and enhanced privacy protection but potentially impacting image clarity and the model’s ability to interpret diagnostic features. Conversely, a lower variance applies subtler noise, which maintains more image detail but offers less privacy shielding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed differential privacy noise injection mechanism ensures that core diagnostic features essential for disease recognition remain intact. The strategic application of Gaussian noise enables the system to comply with data privacy standards while allowing the machine learning model to access sufficient visual information for accurate disease classification. The obfuscated images are suitable for processing by deep learning algorithms, which can learn robust features for pattern recognition even when some noise is present.
This approach is particularly advantageous for applications where image data needs to be shared across different systems or with third parties, maintaining data confidentiality and minimizing the risk of privacy breaches. The flexibility in tuning the variance <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> allows for a customizable level of obfuscation that can be adapted based on specific privacy requirements or regulations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Deep Learning-based Crop Disease Classification</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After acquiring the obfuscated crop images dataset, we have developed a lightweight deep learning model tailored for crop disease classification. This model is structured as follows:
(1) Data Normalization: An essential preprocessing step, data normalization adjusts each pixel value by dividing by 255. This scales the image pixel values to a range of [0, 1], ensuring a uniform distribution of input values. Such normalization not only stabilizes the learning process but also accelerates convergence and improves the accuracy of the model by minimizing the impact of variations in image brightness and contrast.
(2) Convolutional and Pooling Layers: These layers are crucial for extracting spatial and local features from the images of rice diseases. The convolutional layers detect these features, while the pooling layers reduce the dimensionality of the data, which helps in handling overfitting.
(3) Dense and Activation Layers: Positioned after the convolutional and pooling layers, the dense layer, coupled with a ReLU activation layer in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.2. Deep Learning-based Crop Disease Classification ‣ 3. AgriSentinel Model ‣ AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), integrates the extracted features. This combination is pivotal for the complex decision-making required to accurately predict disease categories.
By streamlining the architecture with these elements, our model remains lightweight yet powerful enough to effectively classify various crop diseases based on visual cues.</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=\text{ReLU}(W\cdot x_{obf}+b)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>ReLU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\text{ReLU}(W\cdot x_{obf}+b),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{obf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{obf}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input obfuscated crop dataset, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is the weight matrix learned by convolutional layer and pooling layers during training, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is the bias vector, and <span id="S3.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">ReLU</span> stands for the activation function applied to the output.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This proposed lightweight deep learning model is designed to classify crop images into various disease categories. Central to training this neural network for multi-class classification is the cross-entropy loss function, which measures the discrepancy between the true labels and the model’s predicted probabilities. Categorical cross-entropy effectively gauges how closely the predicted probability distribution matches the actual distribution, providing a clear pathway for the model to refine its predictions. The mathematical formulation of this loss function is presented in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.2. Deep Learning-based Crop Disease Classification ‣ 3. AgriSentinel Model ‣ AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). For optimization, the model employs the Adam optimizer, known for its ability to adjust the learning rate dynamically throughout the training process. The training itself utilizes a fitting method, iteratively adjusting the model’s weights based on the training data to enhance performance and accuracy.</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}=-\sum_{c=1}^{C}y_{c}\log(\hat{y}_{c})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></munderover><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=-\sum_{c=1}^{C}y_{c}\log(\hat{y}_{c}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is the overall loss value that the model aims to minimize during training, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of classes, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is the true label for class <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, which takes a value of 1 if the input image belongs to class <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and 0 otherwise, and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{c}=P(y=c\mid x)=\frac{e^{z_{c}}}{\sum_{j=1}^{C}e^{z_{j}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{c}=P(y=c\mid x)=\frac{e^{z_{c}}}{\sum_{j=1}^{C}e^{z_{j}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the predicted probability for class <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> produced by the softmax output of the model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>On-Device LLM-based Alerting System</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After successfully classifying crop diseases, our system provides precise diagnostic results.
To enhance this functionality, we have established a knowledge pool consisting of question-and-answer pairs <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=(Q,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=(Q,A)</annotation></semantics></math> related to crop disease management, serving as a foundational dataset for fine-tuning an on-device LLM-based alerting system <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AgriLLM(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AgriLLM(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.
When a disease is detected, the model uses the diagnostic results as input, utilizing questions from the knowledge pool as prompts to generate relevant answers.
This integration facilitates the creation of alerts and therapeutic suggestions tailored to the specific disease identified.
The responses <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> generated by the LLM offer concrete advice on managing the disease, providing actionable insights to end-users such as farmers or agronomists.
The process of the proposed on-device LLM-based alerting system can be formulated as below:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=AgriLLM(\hat{y},(Q,A);\theta)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=AgriLLM(\hat{y},(Q,A);\theta),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is the predicted crop disease result, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> represents the parameters of the fine-tuned on-device LLM-based alerting system and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> should be generated as similar as the groundtruth answers <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.
This empowers them with better decision-making tools and effective treatment strategies, optimizing crop health management.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Experiment</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we outline the experimental setup and provide detailed results to demonstrate the effectiveness of our proposed crop disease alerting system in delivering accurate performance while ensuring data privacy protection.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Experiment Settings</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present the experimental setup across several key aspects: dataset selection, data preprocessing, model architecture, training methodology, LLM fine-tuning, and performance evaluation metrics.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1. </span>Dataset</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we use the dataset, including images of rice plants to discern numerous diseases that affect those rice crops <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Narmadha et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
Crop images in the collection are arranged into three primary categories of rice plant diseases in Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1.1. Dataset ‣ 4.1. Experiment Settings ‣ 4. Experiment ‣ AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
(1) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rice Blast</span>: Oval shaped lesions appearing on leaves caused by the fungus Magnaporthe oryzae leads to yield reduction, which is typified by this disease.
(2) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Brown Spot</span>: This disease causes affects of photosynthesis and hence health of the plant caused by the fungus Bipolaris oryzae and appear as dark brown patches on leaves.
(3) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bacterial Leaf Blight</span>: It is caused by a bacterium (Xanthomonas oryzae) that initially manifests as yellow streaks on the leaves, and from which the leaves eventually fall off and production is reduced.
There are totally 1,200 photos in the collection, split equally across the three classifications, and each one is annotated with the ailment it depicts.
In order to facilitate effective access and retrieval throughout the training and evaluation phases, the dataset is arranged into folders, each labeled after the corresponding class.
The dataset was gathered from a variety of agricultural sources and field research, guaranteeing a representative sample of rice illnesses found in different geographic areas.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09103v1/Dataset.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/190;" width="476" height="190" alt="fig:dataset">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Three Primary Categories of Rice Plant Diseases<span id="acmlabel2" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">fig:dataset</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>Different Levels of Privacy Loss</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:69pt;vertical-align:-32.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-14.6pt,2.9pt) scale(0.921824895214925,0.921824895214925) ;">
<table id="S4.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Obfuscation Level</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sensitivity (<math id="S4.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta f</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Noise Scale (<math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy Loss (<math id="S4.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Very High</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.1</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">1.5</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.06</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
