</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2. </span>Data Preprocessing</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Data preparation is a crucial step before feeding images into the proposed lightweight deep learning-based crop disease classification model, ensuring the data is properly formatted for effective training. In this study, all images were resized to 224x224 pixels, a standard size required for CNN models to maintain consistent input dimensions with pre-trained architectures. Pixel values were normalized by dividing each by 255, scaling them to the [0, 1] range, which stabilizes the learning process, accelerates convergence, and improves model accuracy by reducing the impact of brightness and contrast variations. Labels for disease categories were encoded into numerical values, enabling the model to interpret categorical data effectively during training. The dataset was then split into training and testing sets, ensuring 80% of the data was used for training and 20% for testing to evaluate the model’s performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.3. </span>Deep Learning Model Architecture</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed deep learning architecture is designed to effectively classify crop disease images. It begins with an input layer that accepts images of size <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="224\times 224\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>224</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>224</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">224\times 224\times 3</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to the standard RGB format. The first convolutional layer applies 32 filters of size <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> with ReLU activation, followed by a max pooling layer to reduce the spatial dimensions of the feature maps. This is succeeded by a second convolutional layer with 64 filters of the same size and ReLU activation, paired with another max pooling layer. The extracted 2D feature maps are then flattened into a 1D vector by the flattening layer for further processing. A dense layer with 128 units and ReLU activation follows, accompanied by a dropout layer to mitigate overfitting. Finally, the architecture employs a softmax output layer, which calculates class probabilities.</p>
</div>
