<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5. </span>Performance of Our Proposed LLM-based Alerting System</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T5.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:542pt;vertical-align:-269.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-80.2pt,126.0pt) scale(0.68253248885764,0.68253248885764) ;">
<table id="S4.T5.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T5.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T5.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:71.1pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.T5.2.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt (Questions with Predicted Disease Result)</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.T5.2.1.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fine-Tuned LLM’s Answer</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.T5.2.1.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Groundtruth in Knowledge Pool</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T5.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T5.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:71.1pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">How can I prevent <span id="S4.T5.2.1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Brown Spot</span> rice crops in the future?</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">There are several measures you can take to prevent brown spot in rice crops in the future: Plant disease-resistant rice varieties: Choose rice varieties that are resistant to brown spot to reduce the risk of infection. Rotate crops: <span id="S4.T5.2.1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Rotate rice with other crops to reduce the build-up of pathogens in the soil that cause brown spot. Practice good field management:</span> Implement proper irrigation and drainage practices to prevent waterlogging, which can create conditions conducive to brown spot development. Use clean seeds: Plant disease-free seeds to prevent the introduction of pathogens into your rice crop. Apply fungicides: If brown spot is a recurring problem in your area, consider applying fungicides to protect your rice crop from infection. Monitor crop regularly</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">To prevent brown spot in rice crops <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Macasero et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, consider the following measures:
• Plant disease-resistant varieties to reduce infection risk.
• Rotate crops to minimize pathogen build-up in the soil.
• <span id="S4.T5.2.1.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Practice good field management with proper irrigation </span>and drainage to prevent water stress <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Crill et al., 1982</a>)</cite>.
• Use clean seeds to avoid introducing pathogens
• Apply fungicides if brown spot is common in your area.
• Monitor crops regularly for early detection and intervention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguefack et al., 2013</a>)</cite></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T5.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T5.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:71.1pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">What are the environmental conditions that favor rice crops <span id="S4.T5.2.1.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Brown Spot</span>?</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">The environmental conditions that favor rice brownspot, caused by the fungus Bipolaris oryzae, include <span id="S4.T5.2.1.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">warm and humid weather</span>, as well as frequent rainfall or high levels of humidity. These conditions create a suitable environment for the pathogen to thrive and infect rice plants, particularly during the rainy season. Additionally, <span id="S4.T5.2.1.3.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">overcrowded and poorly ventilated fields</span> can also promote the spread of the disease by creating a suitable microclimate for the fungus to grow and spread. Inadequate drainage in the field can also contribute to the development of rice brownspot, as standing water provides a favorable environment for the fungus to infect the plants.</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">•<span id="S4.T5.2.1.3.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Warm and Humid Weather: Optimal for the growth of the Bipolaris oryzae fungus</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singh et al., 2012</a>)</cite>.
• <span id="S4.T5.2.1.3.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Frequent Rainfall or High Humidity</span>: Increases moisture levels, creating favorable conditions for the pathogen.
• Overcrowded Fields: Reduces airflow, creating a microclimate that supports fungal growth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mo et al., 2012</a>)</cite>.
• <span id="S4.T5.2.1.3.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Poor Ventilation</span>: Contributes to moisture retention and a conducive environment for infection.
• <span id="S4.T5.2.1.3.3.1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Inadequate Drainage</span>: Leads to standing water, further supporting fungal development and spread <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fujisaka et al., 1994</a>)</cite>.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T5.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T5.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:71.1pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Factors affecting the life cycle and rice <span id="S4.T5.2.1.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Brown Spot</span> epidemics?</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.4.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">1. Climate: Brown spot disease is more prevalent in areas with warm, humid climates, as these conditions are conducive to the growth and spread of the fungal pathogen responsible for the disease.
2. <span id="S4.T5.2.1.4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Soil type</span>: Certain soil types may be more favorable for the development and spread of brown spot disease, as they provide the ideal conditions for the fungal pathogen to thrive.
3. <span id="S4.T5.2.1.4.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Rice variety</span>: Some rice varieties may be more susceptible to brown spot disease than others. Varieties with genetic resistance to the disease are less likely to be affected.
4. <span id="S4.T5.2.1.4.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Crop rotation</span>: Continuous planting of rice crops in the same field can increase the prevalence of brown spot disease. Rotating rice crops with other crops can help reduce the risk of infection.
5. <span id="S4.T5.2.1.4.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Management practices</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.4.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;">
<span id="S4.T5.2.1.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Factors affecting the life cycle and rice brown spot epidemics include:
1. Climate: Warm, humid conditions support the growth and spread of the Bipolaris oryzae fungus, increasing the risk of epidemics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lirova, 2023</a>)</cite>.
2. <span id="S4.T5.2.1.4.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Soil Type</span>: Poorly drained or low-fertility soils can contribute to disease development.
3. <span id="S4.T5.2.1.4.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Rice Variety</span>: Susceptibility varies; varieties lacking genetic resistance are more prone to infection.
4. <span id="S4.T5.2.1.4.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Crop Rotation</span>: Continuous rice planting increases disease prevalence. Crop rotation with non-host crops helps disrupt the pathogen’s cycle <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berhan Ejigu, 2021</a>)</cite>.
5. <span id="S4.T5.2.1.4.3.1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Management Practices</span>: Poor field management, including suboptimal fertilization and improper irrigation, can create favorable conditions for the disease <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singh and Chawla, 2012</a>)</cite>.</span>
</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6. </span>Performance of Our Proposed LLM-based Alerting System (Continued)</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T6.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:565.6pt;vertical-align:-281.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-80.2pt,131.5pt) scale(0.68253248885764,0.68253248885764) ;">
<table id="S4.T6.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T6.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T6.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:71.1pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Can <span id="S4.T6.2.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Brown Spot</span> spread to other rice crops?</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Yes, <span id="S4.T6.2.1.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Brownspot, a fungal disease caused by Bipolaris oryzae</span>, can spread to other rice crops if proper measures are not taken to control its spread. The disease is commonly spread through wind, rain, and agricultural practices that facilitate the transmission of the fungal spores. To prevent the spread of Brownspot to other rice crops, it is important to implement effective disease management strategies such as <span id="S4.T6.2.1.1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">crop rotation</span>, <span id="S4.T6.2.1.1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">planting resistant varieties</span>, and using fungicides when necessary. Additionally, practicing good sanitation and proper field management can help minimize the spread of the disease.</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.T6.2.1.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Brownspot, a fungal disease caused by Bipolaris oryzae</span>, can spread to other rice crops if not properly managed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berhan Ejigu, 2021</a>)</cite>. The fungus spreads through wind and rain, which carry the spores from infected plants to healthy ones. Agricultural practices, such as using contaminated equipment or not removing infected plant debris, can also facilitate the spread <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parthasarathy et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. To prevent the spread of Brownspot, it is crucial to:
• <span id="S4.T6.2.1.1.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Implement crop rotation with non-host crops.</span>
• Plant disease-resistant rice varieties.
• <span id="S4.T6.2.1.1.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Apply fungicides when necessary and according to local agricultural guidelines</span>
• Practice good field sanitation by removing infected plant material.
• Maintain proper field management, such as adequate spacing for airflow and effective irrigation practices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lirova, 2023</a>)</cite>.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T6.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:71.1pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">What chemicals should I use to treat rice <span id="S4.T6.2.1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Brown Spot</span>?</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">1. Fungicides such as <span id="S4.T6.2.1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">azoxystrobin, flutolanil, or prothioconazole</span> can be used to treat rice Brownspot. These chemicals work by inhibiting the growth of the fungus that causes the disease.
2. Insecticides such as <span id="S4.T6.2.1.2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">chlorpyrifos or malathion</span> may also be used to control insect pests that can contribute to the spread of Brownspot.
3. It is important to carefully follow the application instructions provided on the label of the chemical product to ensure safe and effective treatment of Brownspot in rice. Additionally, it is recommended to rotate between different fungicides with different modes of action to prevent the development of resistance in the fungal population.</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Recommended chemicals for treating rice brownspot include:
1. Fungicides: Use fungicides such as <span id="S4.T6.2.1.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;"> azoxystrobin, flutolanil, or prothioconazole</span>, which are effective in controlling the growth of the <span id="S4.T6.2.1.2.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Bipolaris oryzae</span> fungus.
2. Application Practices:
Always follow the label instructions for safe and effective application.Rotate between fungicides with different modes of action to prevent resistance development in the fungal population <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Van Zyl, 2019</a>)</cite>.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T6.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:71.1pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">How do I improve soil health to prevent rice <span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Brown Spot</span>?</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Improving soil health to prevent rice brown spot:
1. <span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Implement crop rotation</span>: Rotate rice with non-host crops to break the disease cycle and reduce pathogen build-up in the soil.
2. <span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Incorporate organic matter</span>: Add compost or organic matter to improve soil structure, fertility, and biological activity, which makes it less favorable for pathogens.
3. <span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Practice proper irrigation</span>: Avoid over-irrigation and ensure good drainage to prevent waterlogged conditions that can promote pathogen growth.
4. Use disease-resistant varieties: Plant rice varieties that are resistant to brown spot to reduce the risk of infection</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S4.T6.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;">
<span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">1. <span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Crop rotation</span>: This is an effective strategy to disrupt the disease cycle and prevent pathogen build-up. Rotating rice with non-host crops reduces the pathogen load in the soil, making it less likely for diseases like brown spot to persist <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mishra, 2013</a>)</cite>.
2. <span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Incorporate organic matter</span>: Adding compost or organic matter enriches the soil, improving its structure and microbial activity, which can suppress harmful pathogens and promote overall soil health.
3. <span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Proper irrigation practices</span>: Managing water levels to avoid waterlogging is crucial. Over-irrigation creates conditions that can foster pathogen growth. <span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.3.1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Ensuring well-drained</span>fields reduces the risk of brown spot and other diseases that thrive in <span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.3.1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">excessive moisture</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">KHARAYAT, 2023</a>)</cite>.
4. <span id="S4.T6.2.1.3.3.1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Disease-resistant varieties</span>: Planting resistant rice varieties is an effective way to minimize infection risk and reduce reliance on chemical treatments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barnwal et al., 2013</a>)</cite>.</span>
</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
