</span></td>
<td id="S4.T9.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S4.T9.2.1.5.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:341.4pt;">
<span id="S4.T9.2.1.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">A: There are several chemicals that can be used to treat bacterial leaf blight in plants, including Copper-based fungicides: Copper sprays are commonly used to treat bacterial leaf blight as they have strong antibacterial properties and can help prevent the spread of the disease; Streptomycin: Streptomycin is an antibiotic that can be used to treat bacterial leaf blight, particularly in crops such as rice and corn; Oxytetracycline: Oxytetryield losses if left untreated. Bacterial leaf blight is favored by warm and humid conditions, making it a particular problem in regions with tropical or subtropical climates.</span>
</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.4. </span>Deep Learning Model Training</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To conceal sensitive details in the crop images, Gaussian noise will be added using our proposed differential privacy noise injection mechanism, ensuring that the data cannot be reconstructed or exploited by unauthorized users while still enabling the model to learn relevant features for disease detection.
The proposed deep learning-based crop disease classification model then will be trained with the obfuscated crop images.
The training will be performed for a definite batch size of 16 with the early stoppage scheme and learning rate reduction and the training data augmentation techniques were used.
The training for the model continued for several epochs until the resulting validation accuracy stopped increasing to the maximum.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.5. </span>Integration with LLM for Alerts</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We utilize GPT-2 as the foundational LLM model and fine-tune it using our curated knowledge pool on crop disease management and the predicted crop disease results. This fine-tuned, on-device LLM-based alerting system provides farmers with timely crop disease alerts and tailored therapeutic suggestions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.6. </span>Performance Metrics</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use accuracy, precision, recall and F1 score as the metrics for the deep learning performance comparison.
Besides, the metric Privacy Loss <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is used to assess the effectiveness of a privacy-preserving framework in obfuscating sensitive information within images.
In the context of rice disease data, privacy loss quantifies the extent to which identifiable information remains visible after obfuscation.
The calculation of privacy loss is based on two primary factors: (1) Sensitivity of the input <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta f</annotation></semantics></math> and (2) Amount of Noise <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.
Sensitivity measures the maximum change in the output when a single individual’s data is added or removed. In image obfuscation, sensitivity represents the degree of identifiable features retained in the image post-obfuscation.
Noise scale reflects the intensity of obfuscation applied to mask sensitive information. By adding Gaussian noise, the identifiable characteristics of the image are masked, reducing the recognizability of sensitive features.
To compute the overall privacy loss, both sensitivity and noise components are combined in the following manner: <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=\frac{\Delta f}{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mi>σ</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=\frac{\Delta f}{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This equation illustrates that privacy loss <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is directly proportional to the sensitivity <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta f</annotation></semantics></math> and inversely proportional to the noise <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> applied. Consequently, a higher sensitivity or lower noise results in a higher privacy loss, indicating more exposure of identifiable information. Conversely, lower sensitivity or higher noise diminishes privacy loss, thereby strengthening privacy protection.
We show five levels of privacy loss as examples in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1.1. Dataset ‣ 4.1. Experiment Settings ‣ 4. Experiment ‣ AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Evaluation on Privacy-Enhanced Crop Disease Classification Model</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the proposed privacy-enhanced crop disease classification model using various performance metrics under different levels of privacy loss. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1.3. Deep Learning Model Architecture ‣ 4.1. Experiment Settings ‣ 4. Experiment ‣ AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the model without privacy protection serves as the baseline. Compared to the baseline, our model achieves adaptive privacy protection across different levels of privacy loss while maintaining accurate rice blast classification. When the obfuscation level is medium or higher, the model effectively balances data privacy protection and classification performance.
However, as the privacy protection level increases, a slight decline in classification performance is inevitable. Interestingly, we observe that the added noise for privacy protection enhances the robustness of the classification model, improving performance when the privacy protection level is below medium. Additionally, the comparison of classification performance for brown spot and bacterial leaf blight, presented in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.1.3. Deep Learning Model Architecture ‣ 4.1. Experiment Settings ‣ 4. Experiment ‣ AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.1.3. Deep Learning Model Architecture ‣ 4.1. Experiment Settings ‣ 4. Experiment ‣ AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, supports the same conclusion, demonstrating the model’s adaptability and effectiveness across different crop diseases.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Evaluation on LLM-based Alerting System</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After obtaining the crop disease classification results, our LLM-based Alerting System is utilized to address questions related to the identified disease, providing farmers with actionable crop management suggestions. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.1.3. Deep Learning Model Architecture ‣ 4.1. Experiment Settings ‣ 4. Experiment ‣ AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.1.3. Deep Learning Model Architecture ‣ 4.1. Experiment Settings ‣ 4. Experiment ‣ AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, the fine-tuned LLM generates accurate responses closely aligned with expert-provided ground truth knowledge. This demonstrates that the LLM-based alerting system effectively delivers useful and timely suggestions to farmers, offering natural language explanations to support informed decision-making for managing crop diseases.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Case Study of Our Proposed AgriSentinel System</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further demonstrate the effectiveness of our proposed AgriSentinel system through three illustrative examples. In Table <a href="#S4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 4.1.3. Deep Learning Model Architecture ‣ 4.1. Experiment Settings ‣ 4. Experiment ‣ AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, a crop image affected by rice blast is used as input to the AgriSentinel system. First, the image undergoes privacy enhancement using our differential privacy noise injection mechanism. Next, the lightweight deep learning-based crop disease classification model accurately identifies the crop disease in the image. Finally, questions related to the predicted crop disease are addressed by the LLM-based alerting model, providing farmers with actionable crop management suggestions. Additional examples in Table <a href="#S4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 4.1.3. Deep Learning Model Architecture ‣ 4.1. Experiment Settings ‣ 4. Experiment ‣ AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and Table <a href="#S4.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ 4.1.3. Deep Learning Model Architecture ‣ 4.1. Experiment Settings ‣ 4. Experiment ‣ AgriSentinel: Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> highlight the system’s performance for brown spot and bacterial leaf blight diseases. These examples illustrate that AgriSentinel can automatically assist farmers by delivering effective crop disease management advice while safeguarding the privacy of crop images. Furthermore, the system’s lightweight design makes it suitable for deployment on mobile devices, ensuring ease of use and accessibility for farmers.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we propose AgriSentinel, a Privacy-Enhanced Embedded-LLM Crop Disease Alerting System. AgriSentinel integrates a differential privacy noise injection mechanism to protect crop image data, a lightweight deep learning model for accurate crop disease classification, and a fine-tuned LLM to provide farmers with strategic suggestions for crop disease alerting and management. Comprehensive experiments demonstrate that AgriSentinel effectively safeguards crop image privacy while delivering accurate and actionable recommendations for crop disease management. Additionally, the system is lightweight, easy to deploy, and designed to be farmer-friendly, enabling automated crop disease alerting and management, ultimately contributing to improved crop production.</p>
</div>
</section>
